<Docs title="Installation — Matra docs" description="Which packages exist, which are MIT, and which need a subscription." section="Getting started" heading="Installation">
  <p>The core is all most projects need.</p>
  <pre><code>npm i @matrajs/core</code></pre>

  <h2>Framework bindings</h2>
  <pre><code>npm i @matrajs/react   # useEditor, useEditorState, EditorContent
npm i @matrajs/vue     # useEditor, EditorContent</code></pre>
  <p>
    Both are MIT and both are thin — the editor does not care which framework renders around it,
    and neither binding re-implements anything.
  </p>

  <h2>The paid packages</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@matrajs/ai</code> and <code>@matrajs/collab</code> are source-available under a
    commercial licence rather than MIT. Free for evaluation, development, testing, teaching,
    personal projects and teams of fewer than three developers — see
    <a href="/licence">the licence</a>.
  </p>
  <p>A subscription comes with access to the release repository; installing uses it:</p>
  <pre><code>npm i "@matrajs/ai@git+ssh://git@github.com/amrelaco/matra-pro.git#semver:^0.12.0"</code></pre>
  <p>
    They install under their own names, so imports read
    <code>from '@matrajs/ai'</code> exactly as the free packages do. The git URL appears once in
    your manifest and nowhere else, and <code>#semver:</code> resolves against tags, so ranges
    and upgrades work normally.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    Nothing checks a licence at runtime and nothing phones home. Access to the repository is the
    licence taking effect — so if a subscription lapses, whatever you already shipped keeps
    running.
  </div>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Any bundler. The packages ship ESM and CJS with types.</li>
    <li>No peer dependencies for the core.</li>
    <li>Node 18+ for the parts that run on a server — Markdown needs no DOM.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
